  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM EDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nThru Thu...High pressure over the eastern US will maintain a north\nto northeast wind flow across our area. The pressure gradient \nwill support 10-15 mph with gusts 20-25 mph with occasional gusts \nup to 30 mph possible Wed. Less gustiness on Thu. A slug of \nslightly higher PWAT (~1.25\") is forecast Wed which will produce \nmore stratocumulus clouds and an uptick in Atlc showers pushing \nonshore. Drier air makes another push south Thursday, limiting \nrain chances so most of the area will stay dry Thursday. Max temps\nwill be very seasonable Wed in the low to mid 80s, and slightly \ncooler on Thu. Min temps will be generally in the 60s with upper \n50s north of I 4. There is a chance Sanford and/or Orlando may dip\nbelow 60F for the first time this season one of these mornings. \n\nFri-Mon...Northeast pressure gradient re-tightens as high pressure\nbuilds south across the mid Atlc and low pressure develops near or\nNE of the Bahamas along an old frontal boundary. 12z ECM/GFS \nguidance continues to show a 8-10mb delta between JAX/MIA which\nwould produce very windy conditions Sat-Sun. Sustained winds \nFriday afternoon of 15-20 mph increase 20-30 mph Saturday and \nSunday with higher gusts. This would support Wind Advisories at \nleast for the coast. Pressure gradient starts to relax Mon and Tue\nas high pressure weakens allowing winds and gusts to gradually\nsettle down. \n\nThese winds will produce another hazardous coastal event with \nbeach erosion, rough surf, and dangerous rip currents, beginning \nas soon Friday. This event will also coincide with high\nastronomical tides. Rain chances are less certain with the \ngreatest confidence in measurable precip south of Cape Canaveral \nthrough Monday. The best chance for onshore- moving showers \narrives Saturday as a surge of higher PWAT pushes onshore from \nthe Atlantic. Max temps in the upper 70s/low 80s but overnight \nlows warm, holding in the mid 60s interior and low 70s (even mid\n70s) coast due to stronger onshore flow.",
